; ===========================================================================
; WFT FORCE CHANNELS  --  end-to-end capture against a real recording
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The gc_* corpus proved the operators, but every one of those signals was
; generated in Python and merely imported, so our imc file reader was never
; under test. This sequence closes that: FAMOS reads the same .raw files from
; disk that our pipeline reads, and runs the production force chain on them.
;
; The force path is NOT the Latacc path. From the imc config file:
;
;     FL_Fx = smo(FL_Fx1, 0.1)        <- forces and moments: smo only
;     cutdata = red(cutd, 10)
;
; No FiltLP. FiltLP(4, 5 Hz) applies to Latacc alone. Running the accel chain
; on a force channel would validate something the pipeline never does.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; PREREQUISITE -- load these from
;   raw data in .dat format\2006-08-25 09-10-46 (1)\
; They are already FAMOS format, so File > Open > Data opens them directly with
; no import assistant. Each arrives named from inside the file, which is
; already the name used below -- nothing to rename.
;
;   FR_Fx_2.raw   FR_Fy_2.raw   FR_Fz_2.raw
;   RR_Fx_1.raw   RR_Fy_1.raw   RR_Fz_1.raw
;
; If memory or time runs short, load the three FR channels only and comment out
; the RR block. Fz is the single most valuable one: it carries the static wheel
; load, so an error there moves every severity and histogram figure.
; ===========================================================================


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; STAGE F1: smo(0.1) -- the force smoothing, on its own
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Isolated so a mismatch can be attributed. Combined with red() below, a
; disagreement could come from either operator or from the reader; separated,
; each one answers for itself.

FR_Fx_2_smo = smo(FR_Fx_2, 0.1)
FR_Fy_2_smo = smo(FR_Fy_2, 0.1)
FR_Fz_2_smo = smo(FR_Fz_2, 0.1)

RR_Fx_1_smo = smo(RR_Fx_1, 0.1)
RR_Fy_1_smo = smo(RR_Fy_1, 0.1)
RR_Fz_1_smo = smo(RR_Fz_1, 0.1)


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; STAGE F2: smo(0.1) -> red(10) -- the full production chain
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; This is exactly what a force column in processed_data.csv is, before the unit
; handling. Matching here is what "our processed output matches FAMOS" means.

FR_Fx_2_red = red(FR_Fx_2_smo, 10)
FR_Fy_2_red = red(FR_Fy_2_smo, 10)
FR_Fz_2_red = red(FR_Fz_2_smo, 10)

RR_Fx_1_red = red(RR_Fx_1_smo, 10)
RR_Fy_1_red = red(RR_Fy_1_smo, 10)
RR_Fz_1_red = red(RR_Fz_1_smo, 10)


; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; STAGE F3: the raw channel decimated with no smoothing
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Reads the file through FAMOS with no filtering at all, so any difference is
; the READER alone -- byte offsets, event-record stripping, the leading and
; trailing sample trim. If F1 and F2 disagree but this matches, the fault is in
; an operator; if this disagrees too, it is in how the file is being read, and
; no amount of operator work will fix it.

FR_Fz_2_rawred = red(FR_Fz_2, 10)
RR_Fz_1_rawred = red(RR_Fz_1, 10)
